## Scope
Audit the pull request for security risk. Focus on authentication, authorization, secret handling, replay resistance, data exposure, dependency risk, injection surfaces, filesystem and network boundaries, and unsafe operational defaults.

Ground each finding in evidence from the diff or nearby code. When no issue is found, state the residual risk and any coverage gaps.

## Submit
Write the security audit to `{output_target}`. Lead with high-impact findings, then note tests or checks performed and any required remediation.

## Submit formal review
After writing `{output_target}`, you MUST submit the formal Forgejo review. This is the deliverable; an artifact file alone is not complete.

Map your final decision to the CLI event: `APPROVED` -> `approved`; any blocking finding or required remediation -> `changes`.

Run this command, replacing `<approved|changes>` with the mapped event and never printing tokens:

```bash
"$AGENTKAI_HOME/workspace/agent-ops-forgejo-taskboard-cli.py" --org {forgejo_org} \
  pr-review {forgejo_repo} {pr_number} <approved|changes> "Security Auditor" --body-file "{output_target}"
```

Then verify the review landed:

```bash
"$AGENTKAI_HOME/workspace/agent-ops-forgejo-taskboard-cli.py" --org {forgejo_org} \
  fg-reviews {forgejo_repo} {pr_number}
```

The verification must show a review by the Security Auditor role identity (`agent-security-auditor`). Report the review id. If posting or verification fails, treat the run as a hard failure and do not report success.

